# #1977: a sandbox descriptor that abuses a private-loop-counter opcode must not
# corrupt the HOST frame. OP_LOOP_EXIT_NAMED (101) drains every operand-stack
# slot above its depth operand; the verifier modelled it as a fixed one-pop, so
# a depth-0 EXIT_NAMED verified clean and then drained past the frame. Standalone
# it double-freed the host's value (SIGABRT); nested inside a host `loop while`
# it removed the host loop's own private counter, so the host failed with
# "loop counter depth 0 out of range". The verifier now refuses the opcode in
# assembled bytecode. This runs under all three execution tiers because the host
# loop (whose counter is the target) is JIT/OSR-compilable.
bad is [1, [2, 2, 101, 0, 0, 35, 41], []]

# Standalone: refused, ok=false, no crash.
r is sandbox_run of [bad, 5]
print of ["standalone", r["ok"]]

# Nested inside a host loop whose own private counter is live on the stack: the
# host loop must run to completion and publish its own (uncorrupted) count.
i is 0
loop while i < 3:
    i is i + 1
    inner is sandbox_run of [bad, 5]
print of ["host", i, __loop_iterations__, inner["ok"]]
